{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"Get-Content docs/work/active/wi-961/WI-961-subagent-usage-ingest.md; Get-Content docs/rubrics/kit-change-review.md; git diff --stat 5db53a6b31601a117ebc12502cf599dd137d5772...5493669d4a43161a2fdec10aa629def6a58d550a -- . ':(exclude)docs/iteration' ':(exclude)docs/reviews' ':(exclude)docs/decisions' ':(exclude)docs/plans/*/critique-*' ':(exclude)docs/log.md' ':(exclude)docs/log.d' ':(exclude)PROJECT_STATE.html' ':(exclude)docs/open-items.html' ':(exclude)docs/stage'; rg -n '\"'^## |''^### |Review threat|When a guard|Consolidate'\"' project-trajectory/PROCESS.md\"","aggregated_output":"+++\r\nid = \"WI-961\"\r\ntitle = \"Claude coordinator sessions and their sub-agents, nested agents included, are recorded once per invocation from their transcripts, under the usage ledger's invocation key\"\r\nworkstream = \"process\"\r\nsr_refs = [\"SR-222\"]\r\nspecref = \"docs/plans/2026-10-11-review-churn/README.md#8-record-usage-for-every-model-call-sub-agents-included\"\r\nbuildtier = \"strong\"\r\nsafety_class = \"ordinary\"\r\npriority = 5\r\nplanmode = \"single\"\r\nneeds = [\"WI-960\"]\r\n+++\r\n\r\n## Context\r\n\r\nReview-churn item 8, proposal steps 2 and 3, with revision 37's widening:\r\nplanners, builders and reviewers may spawn sub-agents, so nested agents are\r\nin scope (queue row P2, second half). This row is split from WI-800. It\r\ntakes WI-800's ledger contract (one usage record per invocation, keyed by\r\ninvocation id, appended once), not WI-800's store. WI-960 is the first half.\r\n\r\n**Size of the gap** (plan item 8, leg 3, counted once per message id):\r\nbuilders were about 74% of the leg's Claude output, and all sub-agents about\r\n80%. None of it is in the kit's records. The coordinator session itself\r\n(attended, or a headless `claude -p` leg: `overnight_legs.py` launches it\r\nwith `--output-format text`, so no result usage is captured) is not recorded\r\neither.\r\n\r\n**Where the data is: verified on this box, 2026-10-20** (Claude Code; the\r\nreads are in NOTES):\r\n- A session's transcript is `~/.claude/projects/<cwd slug>/<session\r\n  id>.jsonl`. Attended (`entrypoint` `claude-vscode`) and headless\r\n  (`sdk-cli`) sessions both write there. The slug's drive-letter case\r\n  varies (`c--Projects-...` and `C--Projects-...` are one directory on\r\n  NTFS).\r\n- Every sub-agent of that session, **depth 2 included**, is a flat file\r\n  `<session id>/subagents/agent-<agentId>.jsonl` beside\r\n  `agent-<agentId>.meta.json`. The meta names `agentType`, `description`,\r\n  `toolUseId`, `spawnDepth` (1 or 2 observed: 462 and 22 metas), and, for\r\n  depth 2, `parentAgentId`, the depth-1 agent in the same directory. A\r\n  `model` key appears only when the spawner overrode it. No depth-3 agent\r\n  was observed.\r\n- Each assistant message's `usage` repeats across records: one depth-2\r\n  transcript held 86 assistant records for 41 message ids. Count once per\r\n  `message.id`.\r\n- The parent's tool result for a sub-agent carries a `usage` that is **not**\r\n  the sub-agent's total: one depth-1 agent's summary reported 633 output\r\n  tokens against 53,412 summed over its 20 unique messages. The summary is\r\n  never a source.\r\n- A sub-agent's environment carries `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID`, equal to its\r\n  parent session's id.\r\n- A session launched under a declared account home (`CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`,\r\n  WI-798) writes under that home's `projects/`: the ADJUDICATOR home\r\n  (`%APPDATA%/project-trajectory/accounts/ADJUDICATOR/projects/`) holds\r\n  `C--Projects-ai-template` and lane slugs. `session_keep.account_home`'s\r\n  docstring says \"the kit never reads, moves, copies or deletes what a\r\n  provider writes there\". LLR-336 (approved) narrows that to \"provider\r\n  authentication state is never read ...; Codex rollout telemetry is the\r\n  LLR-267 exception\". So this row reads the ambient home only. Reading an\r\n  account home's transcripts would need an LLR-336 exception like the codex\r\n  one (NOTES, call C6).\r\n\r\n**Not verified** (the lane's first step, recorded in its decisions before\r\nany coverage claim): whether a resumed session (`--resume`, or `SendMessage`\r\nto a finished agent) appends to the same file or starts another; whether a\r\n`claude -p --output-format json` result's usage includes its sub-agents\r\n(plan step 3; if it does, a service-launched Claude call's sub-agents are\r\nalready counted and must not be counted again); and whether any depth-3\r\nagent can exist.\r\n\r\n**Anchor.** SN-026 (approved) through SR-222 (approved, derived). SR-222\r\nbinds the sessions the loop launches. A session the coordinator's own\r\nharness or an agent's tool spawns is not launched by the kit, so the lane\r\ndrafts one derived SR under SN-026, performance lens, with a TC per arm. In\r\ncapability voice (ruling 5): \"every model session spent on the project's\r\ncoordinated work, including sessions an agent spawns within its own session\r\nat any nesting depth, is recorded once in the usage record, and a repeated\r\nor resumed recording adds nothing\". Its LLR states the reader's refusal on\r\nan unknown transcript shape.\r\n\r\n## Deliverables\r\n\r\n1. **The reader, in one adapter.** The transcript format belongs to Claude\r\n   Code (plan item 8, \"Trade-off\"), so its reader lives beside the claude\r\n   adapter in `session_adapters` and is the only code that knows the shape.\r\n   For one session it yields the session itself and each sub-agent at any\r\n   depth. Each comes with its identity (session id, agent id, parent agent\r\n   id, depth, agent type), its model, its usage summed once per message id\r\n   in the vocabulary SR-222 pins (`usage_record` keys, input inclusive of\r\n   cache), the message ids counted, and its first and last timestamps.\r\n   It refuses, naming the file and the field, on a record shape it does not\r\n   know. It never skips one silently.\r\n2. **The invocation identity and exactly-once.** The ledger contract's\r\n   minimal part, carried here because WI-800's ledger does not exist yet:\r\n   - Each ingested invocation's `invocation-id` is derived from its\r\n     transcript identity (session id and agent id), not from `uuid4`.\r\n   - The log records the message ids it counted, as a count and a cursor,\r\n     the last message id. The cursor is a column of the log, no new carrier.\r\n   - A second ingest over the same transcript records only messages after\r\n     the recorded cursor (a resumed transcript) or nothing (a repeated\r\n     close-out), reading the logs already recorded, committed and spooled.\r\n   - Each log also carries a contribution key, immutable and bound to\r\n     the message range it counted (the invocation identity with the\r\n     first and last message ids counted), so a resumed transcript's\r\n     later log is a second contribution to the same invocation, never\r\n     a duplicate of the first.\r\n   - WI-800's ledger folds each contribution once, keyed by its\r\n     contribution key, into its invocation's total (WI-800's\r\n     Done-when, amended at this row's filing).\r\n3. **Recording through the service's writer.** Each ingested invocation is\r\n   one session log written by `session_service.record`. It carries a role\r\n   of its own (`SUBAGENT` for a sub-agent, `COORDINATOR` for the session\r\n   itself; never `BUILD`, `REVIEW-A`, `REVIEW-B` or empty) and the standard\r\n   `call_` log name. Run from the primary checkout at close-out, it\r\n   therefore spools under WI-960's out-of-lane arm. The log body is a usage\r\n   summary only, never the transcript: prompts and outputs stay out of\r\n   tracked records (SR-176). The usage source is marked as read from a\r\n   transcript (`usage-source`), not reported by a launch.\r\n4. **The command and its place in the cycle.** One command ingests the\r\n   current session (`CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID`) or a named one, from the\r\n   ambient home's `projects/` (account homes stay unread, per LLR-336,\r\n   unless call C6 rules otherwise). The coordinator runs it at close-out\r\n   (`coordinator-cycle` §6) and a headless leg at its end (§7). It skips the\r\n   main transcript of a session the service already recorded, matched by\r\n   the log's `session-id`, so an adjudicator call is not counted twice.\r\n   Whether it skips that session's sub-agents follows the plan-step-3\r\n   finding above.\r\n5. **Records.** IF-245's contract body (the reader), the command's\r\n   interface row, the derived SR, its LLR and TCs, and one RESYNC entry\r\n   (a new adapter and command in shipped modules).\r\n\r\n## Trust\r\n\r\nIngested logs land where a gate reads session logs (`docs/iteration/`, via\r\nthe spool and WI-800's harvest). This row's ruling keeps them out of every\r\ngate. Written under [PROCESS.md §3, \"When a guard is owed\"](../../../../project-trajectory/PROCESS.md#3-traceability--anti-duplication).\r\n\r\n- **Producer:** the ingest command, through `session_service.record`. Its\r\n  input is Claude Code's transcript files, which neither the kit nor the\r\n  owner writes. Failed writes: a log spooled for some invocations and not\r\n  others (killed mid-run), or a spool write that raises.\r\n- **Consumers** of the logs it writes (grep; the list WI-960's Trust gives):\r\n  `project-trajectory/scripts/kitlib/verdict.py` (`session_log`,\r\n  `review_logs`, `logged_rounds`, the integrator's verdict gate), which\r\n  reads only `SESSION_LOG_RE` names; `project-trajectory/scripts/agent_common.py`\r\n  `phase_draw_ordinal` (skips `call_`) and `next_session_number`;\r\n  `project-trajectory/scripts/agent_brief.py` `last_build_family` (phase\r\n  `BUILD` or empty only); this row's own exactly-once read; WI-800's\r\n  harvest and ledger once built.\r\n- **Ruling:**\r\n  - The ingested logs carry no gate authority: a `call_` name and a role\r\n    outside `BUILD`, `REVIEW-A`, `REVIEW-B` and empty, so no consumer above\r\n    reads one as a round, a build or a draw.\r\n  - A transcript that is absent: nothing recorded for it, and the command\r\n    names it. Unreadable, or of an unknown shape: the command exits nonzero\r\n    naming the file and field, and records nothing for that transcript. It\r\n    never records a partial sum as complete.\r\n  - A sub-agent whose meta is missing or malformed: refused the same way.\r\n    A depth-2 agent whose `parentAgentId` names no agent in the directory:\r\n    recorded with its parent unresolved and the command says so. Its usage\r\n    is real and is never dropped.\r\n  - A killed run is recovered by the next run through exactly-once\r\n    (Deliverable 2): what was recorded is not repeated; what was not is\r\n    recorded.\r\n  - The cursor binds to the logs already recorded, committed or spooled.\r\n    An unreadable earlier log: the command refuses, rather than re-count\r\n    or skip.\r\n- **The bound** (PROCESS.md §6): transcripts as Claude Code writes them\r\n  under the ambient home. A hand-edited transcript, and a session run under\r\n  a declared account home (the adjudicator's), are outside the promise\r\n  unless call C6 widens it. The adjudicator's own calls are recorded by the\r\n  service already.\r\n\r\n## Done-when\r\n\r\nEach named test fails on the code before the change.\r\n- **The reader**, on fixtures cut from real transcripts (usage values kept,\r\n  text removed): a session with one depth-1 and two depth-2 agents yields\r\n  four invocations with their identities. Each usage equals the sum over\r\n  unique message ids. Repeated records add nothing. The parent's tool-result\r\n  summary is not read.\r\n- **Unknown shape.** A transcript record or meta missing a field the reader\r\n  needs: nonzero exit, file and field named, nothing recorded for it.\r\n- **Exactly once.** Ingesting one session twice records nothing the second\r\n  time. Appending messages to a fixture transcript and ingesting again\r\n  records only the appended messages, under the same invocation identity.\r\n  A run killed after some logs were spooled, then re-run, ends with each\r\n  invocation recorded once. An initial ingest, a resumed transcript's\r\n  ingest and a repeated ingest give two logs under one invocation\r\n  identity with distinct contribution keys, whose usage summed equals\r\n  the usage over every unique message id.\r\n- **No double count.** A session whose id a service-recorded log already\r\n  carries: its main transcript is skipped; its sub-agents follow the\r\n  recorded plan-step-3 finding.\r\n- **No gate authority.** An ingested log in a lane's `docs/iteration/` is\r\n  not a round to `logged_rounds`, not a build to `last_build_family`, and\r\n  not counted by `phase_draw_ordinal`.\r\n- **No transcript text** in any written log (a planted prompt string\r\n  appears 0 times).\r\n- The three unverified facts are recorded in the lane's decisions with the\r\n  probe that settled each, before the coverage claim in the derived SR is\r\n  judged.\r\n- The coordinator-cycle close-out (§6) and headless-leg (§7) text runs the\r\n  command; all three skill copies byte-identical.\r\n- One live ingest of a real coordinator session with sub-agents has run,\r\n  and its totals are compared with the plan's leg-3 method.\r\n- On the landing tip, the commit bar is green, `check_trajectory.py\r\n  --strict` and `trace.py --strict` are clean, the derived rows are judged\r\n  at the lane's sitting, and the RESYNC entry anchors at the landing's\r\n  parent.\r\n\r\n## Scope answers\r\n\r\n- **S1.** One deliverable: the ingest. It is the second of item 8's two and\r\n  lands after WI-960. Plan step 3 (does a `-p` result include its\r\n  sub-agents) is folded in as a verification, not a deliverable. Plan step\r\n  4 (per-WI reports from the rows) is a query over the ledger. It needs\r\n  WI-800's ledger and is not filed here.\r\n- **S2.** `## Trust`: the logs land in a gate-read directory; the ruling\r\n  keeps them out of every gate.\r\n- **S3.** Closed: the transcript shapes the reader knows, every other shape\r\n  refused; depth by the meta's `spawnDepth`, any value accepted and\r\n  recorded, with the parent resolved or named unresolved; exactly-once by\r\n  invocation identity and cursor.\r\n- **S4.** `single`: a Trust section, an input space (a third party's\r\n  format), two script families (`session_adapters`, the command and its\r\n  caller in the cycle text).\r\n- **S5.** A new input (Claude Code's transcripts), a new value in the role\r\n  domain (`SUB
[... byte cap hit — full stream in out/run-logs/ ...]
cation (the preamble above; `docs/process.md` §4), which takes it\\n  in the same commit as its own ruling on the amendment.\\n\\n## 6. Cell hygiene — a registry holds living truth\\n\\nTiering decides *which row* says a thing. These decide *what a cell may hold at\\nall*, and they cut across every tier. Each is cheap to violate and expensive to\\nfind later, because the mechanized detectors mostly read PROSE and these\\nfailures hide in FIELDS.\\n\\n- **A cell states what is true now — never when it changed.** Provenance has\\n  homes that cannot drift: git, the log's decisions, the archive. A field\\n  recording an amendment date is the same defect the stand-alone rule forbids in\\n  prose, and it survives only because the checker reads text, not schema. If you\\n  are about to add `amended`, `updated`, `since` or a version stamp to a\\n  registry row, the fact already exists somewhere better.\\n- **The reason cell is not a changelog.** `Rationale` (`why` at `SN`) is the one\\n  cell whose whole job is argument, which is why every citation drifts into it —\\n  and it is covered by the rule above, on all four spine tiers. It states **what\\n  breaks without the row** and **which alternative lost**, and carries no citation\\n  frame: no work-item id, no ruling, sitting, review-round or open-item reference,\\n  no decision id, no `AMENDED`/`REWORDED`/`MINTED` verb, no date stamp. Those\\n  belong in the log, which can hold the full account and cannot rot into the\\n  specification.\\n- **When you strip a frame, keep the reason.** The failure mode is deleting\\n  `AMENDED 2026-03-04 (round 2, finding F7): the cell claimed a speedup nothing\\n  measures` in one stroke — frame *and* argument — leaving a bare assertion.\\n  Restate the durable half as standing prose (\\\"this states a structural property,\\n  not a throughput claim: no instrument here measures speedup\\\") and send the rest\\n  to the log. If deleting the frame leaves nothing, the cell was a changelog and\\n  the log already holds it; delete the whole block. `scripts/trace.py` reports\\n  what is left as a **worklist**, warn-first — a row whose frame is the only\\n  record of an unresolved question gets a reviewed entry in the detector's\\n  allow file rather than a silent deletion.\\n- **A cell is not a receipt — test it on a reader with no history.** A cell\\n  written just after a correction tends to answer the version it replaced (\\\"no\\n  longer counts drafts\\\", \\\"now read from the registry\\\", \\\"only the declared\\n  set\\\"): it is a receipt for the fix, and it reads as a rule only to someone\\n  who saw the fix. The test is cheap: hand the cell alone to a fresh context\\n  and ask it to restate what the row requires. If its restatement reconstructs\\n  the correction (\\\"so it used to count drafts\\\"), the cell carries provenance;\\n  rewrite it as what holds, and put the correction in the log.\\n- **One vocabulary per axis, across every tier.** If three tiers say `Drafted`\\n  and the fourth says something else for the same state, a reader must learn a\\n  different field per tier and every cross-tier query grows a special case.\\n  Reach for the vocabulary that exists before minting a parallel one — a new\\n  marker for a state the spine already names is a synonym, not a feature.\\n- **Do not declare what the row already derives.** If a field's value is a\\n  function of the other cells — a type implied by which fields are present, an\\n  owner implied by a link — it is a second source that can disagree with the\\n  first. A redundant cell can be deleted; a *disagreeing* one cannot, and you\\n  will not know which you have until it disagrees.\\n- **One question per field.** A field answering two unrelated questions (a\\n  maturity state and a row type in one `kind`) cannot be queried for either\\n  without knowing the other, and neither half can change vocabulary\\n  independently.\\n- **Prefer the smallest closed vocabulary that stays honest.** Enum values are\\n  cheap to add and near-impossible to remove once rows carry them; a value whose\\n  meaning overlaps an existing one will be applied inconsistently from the day\\n  it lands.\\n\\r\\n# Spine authoring in a lane\\n\\nUse the mode table and parent rule in [../SKILL.md](../SKILL.md), \\\"Three ways a\\nspine grows\\\". The tier questions in §1–§3 apply in both lane modes.\\n\\n## In a lane, released rungs: one change set, authored and judged whole\\n\\nWhy closed and whole: an open set converges one missing link per sitting (a\\nchild returned for want of a parent, then a TC for an arm its LLR states).\\n\\n- **Name the anchor first.** This is the approved need or requirement the\\n  change hangs from. An additional obligation derived through a lens needs a\\n  labelled derived SR (§2(c)); a missing stakeholder outcome needs an SN\\n  proposal, not an unrelated parent.\\n- **Author the subtree in one pass.** Include every new and amended row from\\n  the anchor down (SRs, LLRs, TCs and interface rows) and each approved row\\n  whose text the change makes untrue. New rows stay `Drafted`; interface rows\\n  carry no approval status. Leave existing statuses unchanged while authoring.\\n- **Author it before the build.** TC rows are authored with their LLRs before\\n  the build, so the build's first deliverable is the tests they name. Their\\n  trace fields (TC `Evidence`, LLR `Module` and symbols) name the tests and\\n  symbols the build will create, and bind when the set is reconciled. Row\\n  text is still reconciled and judged against settled code, as below.\\n- **Map the arms.** For each LLR, list what it states (each outcome, refusal\\n  and exemption) and the TC clause that verifies each. Structural checks do\\n  not establish this coverage.\\n- **Amend the row text against settled code.** While the code is still\\n  changing, keep only the trace fields current (`Implements:`, LLR `Module`\\n  and symbols, TC `Evidence`) and list the row-text changes the code implies.\\n  Write and judge them once the code stops moving: each text change made\\n  after a sitting owes another (`docs/process.md` §4, \\\"sequence\\n  requirement-text work *into* an open window\\\"). A Done-when edit is the\\n  exception: it holds the lane's next build until its exact text is blessed.\\n- **Reconcile before judgement.** Check the whole set against the code and\\n  its trace fields before the sitting.\\n- **Close the edges before the sitting.**\\n  - Each row's parent is in the set or already approved.\\n  - Each TC names its LLR and that LLR's SR.\\n  - Each interface row the change adds or alters has a citing TC.\\n  - `scripts/check_trajectory.py --strict` passes, and so does\\n    `scripts/trace.py --strict` from DevStg-Impl. Below that rung\\n    `trace.py --strict-integrity` stands in, because an SR still lacking its\\n    LLR or TC is the rung's own open work; that is also why the harness's\\n    traceability step starts at DevStg-Impl. They check the whole repo, so\\n    judge arm coverage separately using the map.\\n- **Judge it in one sitting, top-down.** One combined adjudication (the\\n  `combined` brief) takes the set's amendments, first approvals and any\\n  Done-when change together. Keep each kind's verdict lines in its own section\\n  of one verdict file; commit it before each kind's act, taken in its own\\n  reviewed commit. Read parents before children and approve parents first.\\n  If a parent is returned, withhold its children's approval and distinguish\\n  chain-only returns from defects in the children's own text.\\n\\n## In a lane, a held rung: split the set at the dial\\n\\nUse the same authoring and closure checks, then split approval authority:\\n\\n- **Held first drafts stay `Drafted`.** Include them as chain evidence; their\\n  approval stays with the owner through the approval brief.\\n- **Judge held amendments for CLARITY or MEANING.** CLARITY means the obligation\\n  is unchanged. Only an independent sitting that rules the amendment CLARITY\\n  in its verdict may re-attest it; name that file in the act's `--verdict`.\\n  A MEANING change awaits the owner. Leave existing statuses unchanged.\\n- **Released children wait for their held parent.** They may be judged in the\\n  same sitting, but approved only after the parent is approved for its current\\n  text.\\n\\nIf the snapshot refuses because another approved row in the registry still\\ndrifts, leave it unanchored and report the refusal; do not widen the act to\\ninclude text you have not judged (`docs/process.md` §4; amendment brief).\\n\\r\\n---\\nname: session-protocol\\ndescription: Use when running a work-item (WI) or thread session in this template repo — how to read the plan, execute only the scoped work, pass the gates, write the session/WI log, and commit in this repo's style.\\nstacks: [any]\\ndomains: [any]\\nphases: [dev, gate, release]\\ntags: [session, improvement-plan, workflow, commit-style, wi]\\nscope: this-repo\\n---\\n\\n# Session protocol (this template repo)\\n\\nHow a WI/thread session runs here. The live homes: **`docs/status.md`** (what's\\nnext), **`docs/work/`** (the WI registry — one spec file per item, status = its\\ndirectory), and\\n**`docs/log.md`** (the session/gate record). This skill is the fast path; the\\nprocess masters (`PROCESS.md` / `PROCESS_OPTIONS.md`) win when they disagree. The\\nkit's design history — the old thread specs and the WI-1.x log — is archived at\\n`docs/archive/IMPROVEMENT_PLAN.md` (context, not a working surface).\\n\\n## 1. Read before doing\\n\\n- Read `CLAUDE.md` (governs editing the kit) and `AGENTS.md` (the pointer stub).\\n- Read `docs/status.md` (the working surface — what's next) and the scoped WI's\\n  spec file under `docs/work/` (its directory is its status). The\\n  spec-of-record for a WI is what its `specref` points at (an SR and/or a plan doc); older landed work is in the\\n  archived plan. **Do only the scoped work** — no unrelated edits.\\n- If a stub is being revived, find and link its earlier backlogged form (search\\n  `docs/archive/scratch.md` + the stub threads) so the resolution is traceable.\\n- **Check `git status` first.** Residue in the working tree is from an\\n  interrupted session; reconcile it against the open WI's spec / Done-when\\n  *before* new work — verify-and-commit what is complete, discard what is not\\n  part of the scope, and record which in the log. (The unattended loop surfaces\\n  this into the session prompt; the judgment is yours — it never auto-stashes.)\\n- **Treat a declared-policy change as a status-staleness event.** In the same\\n  sitting, grep `docs/status.md` for pause/stop/approval\\n  language predicated on the old `gate_policy`, `push`, `review_rounds`, or\\n  `guardrails` value; point to `docs/process.toml` instead of paraphrasing it.\\n\\n## 2. Respect the constraints\\n\\n- **Byte budgets** on `AGENTS.template.md` (10,000) and `PROCESS.md` — see the\\n  `byte-budget-guard` skill. Push expansion to `PROCESS_OPTIONS.md` /\\n  `ADOPTING.md` / `EXAMPLE.md`.\\n- **Stdlib-only, cross-platform** kit scripts (Python 3.11+, Windows + POSIX).\\n- **Single source of truth:** state a fact once and link to it; don't paraphrase\\n  a rule into five files.\\n\\n### Standing rules\\n\\nRelocated here from `docs/status.md` (WI-477): they are durable doctrine, and\\nthe working surface is forward-only — what must happen **next**, not what is\\nalways true.\\n\\n- **An id named in `docs/status.md`'s hand-authored prose CANNOT BE CLAIMED.**\\n  `integrate._status_prose_refusal` refuses it at claim time; generated blocks\\n  are exempt. Point at `docs/work/queued/` and let the generated frontier name\\n  ids.\\n- **Never revert a real fix, or sanction a check, to green a step.** Editing a\\n  declared list — a coverage floor, an orphan glob, a ratchet baseline — to\\n  clear a finding IS accepting what it measures. If a ratchet fires on\\n  legitimate work, re-stamp it deliberately and record the reason.\\n- **Signed claims + one-machine humility.** The recurring review-era defect was\\n  signed CLAIMS that pass every test (PROCESS_OPTIONS.md, \\\"Signed\\n  measurements\\\"), and **one machine is one data point** for any OS-behaviour or\\n  timing claim — state the condition, never the universal.\\n- **Measure on a tree whose line endings match the index.** Before trusting any\\n  byte count or hash, run `git ls-files --eol | grep 'w/crlf'` — only\\n  `*.ps1`/`*.cmd`/`*.bat` should appear.\\n- **Claiming runs through the integrator** (`integrate.py claim`); merges are\\n  its serial fail-closed queue, and a pause is a tracked `docs/work/pause`.\\n- **The coordinator's procedure has one home, the `coordinator-cycle` skill**\\n  (adjudication, rendered review briefs, the rework coverage gate, disputes).\\n- **A stopped lane CLOSES; it is never held by renaming its ref (OI-70).** The\\n  only sanctioned stop is the partial close (§4): the spec moves to the terminal\\n  `docs/archive/work/partial/` with a handback report an adjudicator then judges\\n  (queue a successor, or mint an open item). Parking a lane by renaming its\\n  branch ref away from its `docs/work/active/<branch>/` claim is BANNED — a lane\\n  must close or it gets lost, as the wi508 hold nearly was — and\\n  `check_trajectory` reports any active claim with no matching branch ref.\\n\\n## 3. End green (gates)\\n\\nRun the one declared bar command and paste the real output — never a green\\nyou didn't produce:\\n\\n```\\nrun.cmd smoke                                          # Windows launcher\\npython project-trajectory/scripts/run_menu.py smoke    # from a shell\\n```\\n\\nIt must pass before **each** commit — this is the **commit bar**. Its steps\\nare declared once, in `docs/stack.ini` `[run] smoke`, so a build round's bar\\ncannot skip the strict lints it carries (review-churn ruling 6, 2026-10-11;\\nthey never run in the commit hook). The lints run first; the budget check runs\\nlast, and its run is the bar's only tier run.\\n`-m smoke` is the fast per-commit tier; `check_smoke_budget.py --mode warn`\\nruns it once, FAILS the commit on a red tier (with the tier's own exit code),\\nand reports the tier's CPU-seconds and wall time against `docs/stack.ini`\\n`[smoke-budget]`, with a WARN line for a limit over. No bar fails on seconds\\n(the owner, 2026-10-20): `run.cmd audit` judges them, running the same step\\nwith `--mode enforce` against the tier's own CPU-seconds (`cpu-seconds`;\\nother load on the box moves it far less than wall time), a looser wall cap (`wall-seconds`)\\nand the tier's test count (`max-tests`), by hand and at phase close. A breach\\nthere is filed as a re-tier row, never charged to the lane that ran it. The\\ntier stays a real smoke test — \\\"is it basically alive?\\\", not a re-run of\\nmost of the suite. Tiering is\\nopt-out: smoke drops the **subprocess/scaffold-heavy** modules\\n(`tests/conftest.py` `SLOW_MODULES` — \ndocs/iteration/call_b3bfda0651f74f98bcca7b4938c36c18-20261022-191542.log:76: trailing whitespace.\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_4\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"\\\"C:\\\\\\\\Program Files\\\\\\\\PowerShell\\\\\\\\7\\\\\\\\pwsh.exe\\\" -Command \\\"Get-Content docs/work/active/wi-961/WI-961-subagent-usage-ingest.md; Get-Content docs/rubrics/kit-change-review.md; Get-Content project-trajectory/PROCESS.md; git diff --stat 1d6484cc5f0ba2f84c66df0138f3d1856fe1659d...57067b9fc6bf61f4d6c2f02353b38522989ecf49 -- . ':(exclude)docs/iteration' ':(exclude)docs/reviews' ':(exclude)docs/log.md' ':(exclude)docs/log.d' ':(exclude)PROJECT_STATE.html' ':(exclude)docs/open-items.html' ':(exclude)docs/stage'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"+++\\r\\nid = \\\"WI-961\\\"\\r\\ntitle = \\\"Claude coordinator sessions and their sub-agents, nested agents included, are recorded once per invocation from their transcripts, under the usage ledger's invocation key\\\"\\r\\nworkstream = \\\"process\\\"\\r\\nsr_refs = [\\\"SR-222\\\"]\\r\\nspecref = \\\"docs/plans/2026-10-11-review-churn/README.md#8-record-usage-for-every-model-call-sub-agents-included\\\"\\r\\nbuildtier = \\\"strong\\\"\\r\\nsafety_class = \\\"ordinary\\\"\\r\\npriority = 5\\r\\nplanmode = \\\"single\\\"\\r\\nneeds = [\\\"WI-960\\\"]\\r\\n+++\\r\\n\\r\\n## Context\\r\\n\\r\\nReview-churn item 8, proposal steps 2 and 3, with revision 37's widening:\\r\\nplanners, builders and reviewers may spawn sub-agents, so nested agents are\\r\\nin scope (queue row P2, second half). This row is split from WI-800. It\\r\\ntakes WI-800's ledger contract (one usage record per invocation, keyed by\\r\\ninvocation id, appended once), not WI-800's store. WI-960 is the first half.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Size of the gap** (plan item 8, leg 3, counted once per message id):\\r\\nbuilders were about 74% of the leg's Claude output, and all sub-agents about\\r\\n80%. None of it is in the kit's records. The coordinator session itself\\r\\n(attended, or a headless `claude -p` leg: `overnight_legs.py` launches it\\r\\nwith `--output-format text`, so no result usage is captured) is not recorded\\r\\neither.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Where the data is: verified on this box, 2026-10-20** (Claude Code; the\\r\\nreads are in NOTES):\\r\\n- A session's transcript is `~/.claude/projects/<cwd slug>/<session\\r\\n  id>.jsonl`. Attended (`entrypoint` `claude-vscode`) and headless\\r\\n  (`sdk-cli`) sessions both write there. The slug's drive-letter case\\r\\n  varies (`c--Projects-...` and `C--Projects-...` are one directory on\\r\\n  NTFS).\\r\\n- Every sub-agent of that session, **depth 2 included**, is a flat file\\r\\n  `<session id>/subagents/agent-<agentId>.jsonl` beside\\r\\n  `agent-<agentId>.meta.json`. The meta names `agentType`, `description`,\\r\\n  `toolUseId`, `spawnDepth` (1 or 2 observed: 462 and 22 metas), and, for\\r\\n  depth 2, `parentAgentId`, the depth-1 agent in the same directory. A\\r\\n  `model` key appears only when the spawner overrode it. No depth-3 agent\\r\\n  was observed.\\r\\n- Each assistant message's `usage` repeats across records: one depth-2\\r\\n  transcript held 86 assistant records for 41 message ids. Count once per\\r\\n  `message.id`.\\r\\n- The parent's tool result for a sub-agent carries a `usage` that is **not**\\r\\n  the sub-agent's total: one depth-1 agent's summary reported 633 output\\r\\n  tokens against 53,412 summed over its 20 unique messages. The summary is\\r\\n  never a source.\\r\\n- A sub-agent's environment carries `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID`, equal to its\\r\\n  parent session's id.\\r\\n- A session launched under a declared account home (`CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`,\\r\\n  WI-798) writes under that home's `projects/`: the ADJUDICATOR home\\r\\n  (`%APPDATA%/project-trajectory/accounts/ADJUDICATOR/projects/`) holds\\r\\n  `C--Projects-ai-template` and lane slugs. `session_keep.account_home`'s\\r\\n  docstring says \\\"the kit never reads, moves, copies or deletes what a\\r\\n  provider writes there\\\". LLR-336 (approved) narrows that to \\\"provider\\r\\n  authentication state is never read ...; Codex rollout telemetry is the\\r\\n  LLR-267 exception\\\". So this row reads the ambient home only. Reading an\\r\\n  account home's transcripts would need an LLR-336 exception like the codex\\r\\n  one (NOTES, call C6).\\r\\n\\r\\n**Not verified** (the lane's first step, recorded in its decisions before\\r\\nany coverage claim): whether a resumed session (`--resume`, or `SendMessage`\\r\\nto a finished agent) appends to the same file or starts another; whether a\\r\\n`claude -p --output-format json` result's usage includes its sub-agents\\r\\n(plan step 3; if it does, a service-launched Claude call's sub-agents are\\r\\nalready counted and must not be counted again); and whether any depth-3\\r\\nagent can exist.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Anchor.** SN-026 (approved) through SR-222 (approved, derived). SR-222\\r\\nbinds the sessions the loop launches. A session the coordinator's own\\r\\nharness or an agent's tool spawns is not launched by the kit, so the lane\\r\\ndrafts one derived SR under SN-026, performance lens, with a TC per arm. In\\r\\ncapability voice (ruling 5): \\\"every model session spent on the project's\\r\\ncoordinated work, including sessions an agent spawns within its own session\\r\\nat any nesting depth, is recorded once in the usage record, and a repeated\\r\\nor resumed recording adds nothing\\\". Its LLR states the reader's refusal on\\r\\nan unknown transcript shape.\\r\\n\\r\\n## Deliverables\\r\\n\\r\\n1. **The reader, in one adapter.** The transcript format belongs to Claude\\r\\n   Code (plan item 8, \\\"Trade-off\\\"), so its reader lives beside the claude\\r\\n   adapter in `session_adapters` and is the only code that knows the shape.\\r\\n   For one session it yields the session itself and each sub-agent at any\\r\\n   depth. Each comes with its identity (session id, agent id, parent agent\\r\\n   id, depth, agent type), its model, its usage summed once per message id\\r\\n   in the vocabulary SR-222 pins (`usage_record` keys, input inclusive of\\r\\n   cache), the message ids counted, and its first and last timestamps.\\r\\n   It refuses, naming the file and the field, on a record shape it does not\\r\\n   know. It never skips one silently.\\r\\n2. **The invocation identity and exactly-once.** The ledger contract's\\r\\n   minimal part, carried here because WI-800's ledger does not exist yet:\\r\\n   - Each ingested invocation's `invocation-id` is derived from its\\r\\n     transcript identity (session id and agent id), not from `uuid4`.\\r\\n   - The log records the message ids it counted, as a count and a cursor,\\r\\n     the last message id. The cursor is a column of the log, no new carrier.\\r\\n   - A second ingest over the same transcript records only messages after\\r\\n     the recorded cursor (a resumed transcript) or nothing (a repeated\\r\\n     close-out), reading the logs already recorded, committed and spooled.\\r\\n   - Each log also carries a contribution key, immutable and bound to\\r\\n     the message range it counted (the invocation identity with the\\r\\n     first and last message ids counted), so a resumed transcript's\\r\\n     later log is a second contribution to the same invocation, never\\r\\n     a duplicate of the first.\\r\\n   - WI-800's ledger folds each contribution once, keyed by its\\r\\n     contribution key, into its invocation's total (WI-800's\\r\\n     Done-when, amended at this row's filing).\\r\\n3. **Recording through the service's writer.** Each ingested invocation is\\r\\n   one session log written by `session_service.record`. It carries a role\\r\\n   of its own (`SUBAGENT` for a sub-agent, `COORDINATOR` for the session\\r\\n   itself; never `BUILD`, `REVIEW-A`, `REVIEW-B` or empty) and the standard\\r\\n   `call_` log name. Run from the primary checkout at close-out, it\\r\\n   therefore spools under WI-960's out-of-lane arm. The log body is a usage\\r\\n   summary only, never the transcript: prompts and outputs stay out of\\r\\n   tracked records (SR-176). The usage source is marked as read from a\\r\\n   transcript (`usage-source`), not reported by a launch.\\r\\n4. **The command and its place in the cycle.** One command ingests the\\r\\n   current session (`CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID`) or a named one, from the\\r\\n   ambient home's `projects/` (account homes stay unread, per LLR-336,\\r\\n   unless call C6 rules otherwise). The coordinator runs it at close-out\\r\\n   (`coordinator-cycle` §6) and a headless leg at its end (§7). It skips the\\r\\n   main transcript of a session the service already recorded, matched by\\r\\n   the log's `session-id`, so an adjudicator call is not counted twice.\\r\\n   Whether it skips that session's sub-agents follows the plan-step-3\\r\\n   finding above.\\r\\n5. **Records.** IF-245's contract body (the reader), the command's\\r\\n   interface row, the derived SR, its LLR and TCs, and one RESYNC entry\\r\\n   (a new adapter and command in shipped modules).\\r\\n\\r\\n## Trust\\r\\n\\r\\nIngested logs land where a gate reads session logs (`docs/iteration/`, via\\r\\nthe spool and WI-800's harvest). This row's ruling keeps them out of every\\r\\ngate. Written under [PROCESS.md §3, \\\"When a guard is owed\\\"](../../../../project-trajectory/PROCESS.md#3-traceability--anti-duplication).\\r\\n\\r\\n- **Producer:** the ingest command, through `session_service.record`. Its\\r\\n  input is Claude Code's transcript files, which neither the kit nor the\\r\\n  owner writes. Failed writes: a log spooled for some invocations and not\\r\\n  others (killed mid-run), or a spool write that raises.\\r\\n- **Consumers** of the logs it writes (grep; the list WI-960's Trust gives):\\r\\n  `project-trajectory/scripts/kitlib/verdict.py` (`session_log`,\\r\\n  `review_logs`, `logged_rounds`, the integrator's verdict gate), which\\r\\n  reads only `SESSION_LOG_RE` names; `project-trajectory/scripts/agent_common.py`\\r\\n  `phase_draw_ordinal` (skips `call_`) and `next_session_number`;\\r\\n  `project-trajectory/scripts/agent_brief.py` `last_build_family` (phase\\r\\n  `BUILD` or empty only); this row's own exactly-once read; WI-800's\\r\\n  harvest and ledger once built.\\r\\n- **Ruling:**\\r\\n  - The ingested logs carry no gate authority: a `call_` name and a role\\r\\n    outside `BUILD`, `REVIEW-A`, `REVIEW-B` and empty, so no consumer above\\r\\n    reads one as a round, a build or a draw.\\r\\n  - A transcript that is absent: nothing recorded for it, and the command\\r\\n    names it. Unreadable, or of an unknown shape: the command exits nonzero\\r\\n    naming the file and field, and records nothing for that transcript. It\\r\\n    never records a partial sum as complete.\\r\\n  - A sub-agent whose meta is missing or malformed: refused the same way.\\r\\n    A depth-2 agent whose `parentAgentId` names no agent in the directory:\\r\\n    recorded with its parent unresolved and the command says so. Its usage\\r\\n    is real and is never dropped.\\r\\n  - A killed run is recovered by the next run through exactly-once\\r\\n    (Deliverable 2): what was recorded is not repeated; what was not is\\r\\n    recorded.\\r\\n  - The cursor binds to the logs already recorded, committed or spooled.\\r\\n    An unreadable earlier log: the command refuses, rather than re-count\\r\\n    or skip.\\r\\n- **The bound** (PROCESS.md §6): transcripts as Claude Code writes them\\r\\n  under the ambient home. A hand-edited transcript, and a session run under\\r\\n  a declared account home (the adjudicator's), are outside the promise\\r\\n  unless call C6 widens it. The adjudicator's own calls are recorded by the\\r\\n  service already.\\r\\n\\r\\n## Done-when\\r\\n\\r\\nEach named test fails on the code before the change.\\r\\n- **The reader**, on fixtures cut from real transcripts (usage values kept,\\r\\n  text removed): a session with one depth-1 and two depth-2 agents yields\\r\\n  four invocations with their identities. Each usage equals the sum over\\r\\n  unique message ids. Repeated records add nothing. The parent's tool-result\\r\\n  summary is not read.\\r\\n- **Unknown shape.** A transcript record or meta missing a field the reader\\r\\n  needs: nonzero exit, file and field named, nothing recorded for it.\\r\\n- **Exactly once.** Ingesting one session twice records nothing the second\\r\\n  time. Appending messages to a fixture transcript and ingesting again\\r\\n  records only the appended messages, under the same invocation identity.\\r\\n  A run killed after some logs were spooled, then re-run, ends with each\\r\\n  invocation recorded once. An initial ingest, a resumed transcript's\\r\\n  ingest and a repeated ingest give two logs under one invocation\\r\\n  identity with distinct contribution keys, whose usage summed equals\\r\\n  the usage over every unique message id.\\r\\n- **No double count.** A session whose id a service-recorded log already\\r\\n  carries: its main transcript is skipped; its sub-agents follow the\\r\\n  recorded plan-step-3 finding.\\r\\n- **No gate authority.** An ingested log in a lane's `docs/iteration/` is\\r\\n  not a round to `logged_rounds`, not a build to `last_build_family`, and\\r\\n  not counted by `phase_draw_ordinal`.\\r\\n- **No transcript text** in any written log (a planted prompt string\\r\\n  appears 0 times).\\r\\n- The three unverified facts are recorded in the lane's decisions with the\\r\\n  probe that settled each, before the coverage claim in the derived SR is\\r\\n  judged.\\r\\n- The coordinator-cycle close-out (§6) and headless-leg (§7) text runs the\\r\\n  command; all three skill copies byte-identical.\\r\\n- One live ingest of a real coordinator session with sub-agents has run,\\r\\n  and its totals are compared with the plan's leg-3 method.\\r\\n- On the landing tip, the commit bar is green, `check_trajectory.py\\r\\n  --strict` and `trace.py --strict` are clean, the derived rows are judged\\r\\n  at the lane's sitting, and the RESYNC entry anchors at the landing's\\r\\n  parent.\\r\\n\\r\\n## Scope answers\\r\\n\\r\\n- **S1.** One deliverable: the ingest. It is the second of item 8's two and\\r\\n  lands after WI-960. Plan step 3 (does a `-p` result include its\\r\\n  sub-agents) is folded in as a verification, not a deliverable. Plan step\\r\\n  4 (per-WI reports from the rows) is a query over the ledger. It needs\\r\\n  WI-800's ledger and is not filed here.\\r\\n- **S2.** `## Trust`: the logs land in a gate-read directory; the ruling\\r\\n  keeps them out of every gate.\\r\\n- **S3.** Closed: the transcript shapes the reader knows, every other shape\\r\\n  refused; depth by the meta's `spawnDepth`, any value accepted and\\r\\n  recorded, with the parent resolved or named unresolved; exactly-once by\\r\\n  invocation identity and cursor.\\r\\n- **S4.** `single`: a Trust section, an input space (a third party's\\r\\n  format), two script families (`session_adapters`, the command and its\\r\\n  caller in the cycle text).\\r\\n- **S5.** A new input (Claude Code's transcripts), a new value in the role\\r\\n  domain (`SUBAGENT`, `COORDINATOR`), a new usage source (transcript-read).\\r\\n  No new component, no new carrier: the logs and the spool are WI-960's.\\r\\n- **S6.** `WI-960` (hard): its out-of-lane arm is where a close-out ingest's\\r\\n  logs go; without it a trunk-rooted \n5493669d4a43161a2fdec10aa629def6a58d550a\n169:def _count(value):\n594:def _is_count(value):\n633:        if not _is_count(usage.get(key)):\n653:    return value if _is_count(value) else None\n785:    if not _is_count(depth) or depth < 1:\n45:[paths]\r\n49:[product]\r\n64:[tiers]\r\n70:[smoke-budget]\r\n387:# components and holds the view/lifecycle layer rule — and\r\n590:# new in-process module and nothing else - tests/test_gen_components.py, over the\r\n1005:[ci-tiers]\r\n1009:[coverage]\r\n1033:[step:smoke]\r\n1041:[step:doc-refs]\r\n1062:[step:figures]\r\n1088:[dupes-census]\r\n1161:[step:dupes-census]\r\n1171:[step:complexity]\r\n1194:[readability]\r\n1215:[step:readability]\r\n1226:[step:module-coverage]\r\n1251:[arch-map]\r\n1257:[generated]\r\n1311:docs/requirements/components.derived.toml = components\r\n1312:docs/cli-reference.md = cli | <!-- BEGIN GENERATED CLI REFERENCE --> | <!-- END GENERATED CLI REFERENCE -->\r\n1313:docs/interface-reference.md = interface-reference | <!-- BEGIN GENERATED INTERFACE REFERENCE --> | <!-- END GENERATED INTERFACE REFERENCE -->\r\n1319:[agent-loop]\r\n1335:[run]\r\n1353:[bars]\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
